import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.DpMG7kxZ.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"The facilitator","description":"The guarantees verify enforces before any money moves, and what settlement does on Canton.","frontmatter":{"description":"The guarantees verify enforces before any money moves, and what settlement does on Canton."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"facilitator.md","filePath":"facilitator.md"}'),n={name:"facilitator.md"};function s(i,e,d,h,c,l){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="the-facilitator" tabindex="-1">The facilitator <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-facilitator" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The facilitator&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This page zooms in on steps 5 and 6 of <a href="/canton-x402-sdk/pr-111/payment-flow.html">the payment flow</a>, the facilitator&#39;s half. Before any money moves, verify enforces a handful of guarantees — your payment can&#39;t be replayed, tampered with, or under-paid — and settle re-runs all of them before executing on Canton. The facilitator never moves a payer&#39;s funds on its own authority: the only write that moves them is <code>executeAndWaitForTransaction</code> of a <strong>payer-signed</strong> transaction.</p><h2 id="what-verify-guarantees" tabindex="-1">What verify guarantees <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-verify-guarantees" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What verify guarantees&quot;">​</a></h2><p>These run in order, with no chain write. The first six are shared code — the same <code>@chainsafe/x402-core</code> verifier the SDK exposes — so a payer can pre-flight its payload against them. The last is the facilitator&#39;s alone, since only it holds the state involved:</p><ol><li><strong>Right scheme and network</strong> — the payload, the requirements, and the network this facilitator is bound to all agree.</li><li><strong>The transaction decodes</strong> — <code>preparedTransaction</code> is one gzip member within the size caps, its node graph is well-formed (<code>invalid_prepared_transaction_structure</code> otherwise), and it holds the one transfer. Its sender is the payer (the payload names none), and checks 3 to 6 report it. One that does not decode is <code>transfer_mismatch</code> with detail <code>undecodable</code>.</li><li><strong>Still executable</strong> — the signed transfer&#39;s own <code>executeBefore</code> is far enough in the future to settle (<code>transfer_expired</code>). The quote itself no longer expires; the deadline that counts is inside what the payer signed.</li><li><strong>The key belongs to the payer</strong> — the fingerprint derived from the payload&#39;s <code>publicKey</code> must match the <code>::</code> suffix of the transfer&#39;s sender, so a caller can&#39;t sign as a party it doesn&#39;t control.</li><li><strong>The signature is valid</strong> — the hash recomputed from the transaction itself must equal <code>preparedTxHash</code> (<code>prepared_transaction_hash_mismatch</code> otherwise), and the payer&#39;s key must have signed it.</li><li><strong>The signed transfer actually pays you.</strong> This is what binds the payment to your terms: the transfer&#39;s receiver, instrument and amount are checked against the requirements, so a valid signature over a divergent transfer is rejected with <code>transfer_mismatch</code> and the field that diverged. The amount must be EQUAL, not merely sufficient — <code>exact</code> is the scheme&#39;s distinguishing property, and a payer who overpays has no refund path. The transfer&#39;s <code>x402.memo</code> must equal your <code>extra.memo</code> exactly — in both directions, so a transfer carrying a memo you did not quote is refused as well.</li><li><strong>No replay, and you&#39;re an allowed destination</strong> — the payment has not settled before (the ledger is the real guard: a replayed transfer names input holdings that are already spent), and for an authenticated merchant <code>payTo</code> must be a party that key is allowed to receive at (your destination policy). A destination denial comes back as <code>transfer_mismatch</code> with a <code>destination_not_allowed</code> detail on verify, and as <code>unauthorized</code> on settle.</li></ol><h2 id="what-settlement-does" tabindex="-1">What settlement does <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-settlement-does" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What settlement does&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Funds are real Splice <strong>Amulet</strong> (Canton Coin), transferred through the Token Standard&#39;s <code>TransferFactory</code> in one atomic Canton transaction. When your party has a <code>TransferPreapproval</code>, that transfer is direct, so nothing is escrowed or locked up; without one, the Token Standard makes it an offer that completes only once you accept it. What is recorded is the signed transfer&#39;s amount, not the quoted floor, so an overpayment is not understated.</p>',7)])])}const u=t(n,[["render",s]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
